Looking at this code, I see the borrow-checker error. The issue is that `longest` is trying to hold a reference to an element in `items`, but when we iterate over `items` with `for item in items`, we're taking ownership of each element (consuming the vector). Additionally, `longest` is a reference to a `String`, but we're trying to store references to elements that are being moved during iteration.

The fix is to iterate by reference instead of by value, so we don't consume the vector and can safely keep a reference to the longest element.

```rust
fn longest_len(items: Vec<String>) -> usize {
    if items.is_empty() {
        return 0;
    }

    let mut longest = &items[0];
    for item in &items {
        if item.len() > longest.len() {
            longest = item;
        }
    }
    longest.len()
}

fn main() {
    let v = vec![String::from("a"), String::from("bb"), String::from("ccc")];
    println!("{}", longest_len(v));
}
```

